comments:
I did some good progress on the fragmentation issues, I will probably send a PR next week to address the small issues that I uncovered in the stack.

I had an issue with `net_icmpv6_send_error()` where I am not sure about the fix, @jukkar can probably help me since you wrote this piece of code (see commit 899228bb7f8e6a3925f49594185f796942e1e26c). This function was not sending the ICMPv6 error message in some cases. I discovered that it was erroring out after the first call to `net_pkt_write()`. To me, this makes sense, because we are trying to write in overwrite mode in a packet that was just allocated, thus without any valid data. So I did a stupid fix by removing the overwrite mode:

```
--- a/subsys/net/ip/icmpv6.c
+++ b/subsys/net/ip/icmpv6.c
@@ -254,7 +254,7 @@ int net_icmpv6_send_error(struct net_pkt *orig, uint8_t type, uint8_t code,
         * extra space for link addresses so that we can set the lladdr
         * pointers in net_pkt.
         */
-       net_pkt_set_overwrite(pkt, true);
 
        ret = net_pkt_write(pkt, net_pkt_lladdr_src(orig)->addr,
                            net_pkt_lladdr_src(orig)->len);
@@ -281,8 +281,8 @@ int net_icmpv6_send_error(struct net_pkt *orig, uint8_t type, uint8_t code,
        net_pkt_lladdr_src(pkt)->len = net_pkt_lladdr_dst(orig)->len;
        net_pkt_lladdr_dst(pkt)->len = net_pkt_lladdr_src(orig)->len;
 
-       net_pkt_set_overwrite(pkt, false);
-       net_pkt_cursor_init(pkt);
```

and now I am getting the expected ICMPv6 error message in all the cases that I tested. But I am probably missing something, since I do not understand why this overwrite was set. And apparently it worked before in some other cases, since we got some ICMPv6 error messages during the certification attempt (but not all).
